# Proposal: local biomedical image review workspace

**Decision.** Build an offline-first desktop research workspace for six researchers who exchange closed project bundles through ordinary shared storage. It reviews and annotates research images; it is not a diagnostic product. Use a Qt shell with a napari canvas as a replaceable viewing/editing surface, tifffile-backed local TIFF readers, and a deliberately small OME-Zarr v0.5 reader subset. Keep the durable project, coordinate model, source identity, annotation history and export logic application-owned. Start with 2D slice viewing. Treat a multi-gigabyte image as a bounded-access target to measure, not a performance property supplied by any library.

The proposed spike pins are Python 3.12, napari 0.5.6, tifffile 2026.9.20 and Zarr-Python 3.4.0; use Dask only after the integration spike shows that its scheduler helps the chosen readers. These are evidence pins, not a compatible lock or a ship recommendation. The Zarr sparse-coordinate defect described below makes 3.4.0 unsuitable for any unverified annotation path that calls coordinate selection. Do not choose a later package until its release and regression behavior are checked. Pin Qt, Dask, imagecodecs and transitive dependencies after a clean-machine build.

## Revision audit and evidence limits

The current role manifest admits the same-arm frozen research proposal and its source, witness and lead catalogs. It contains no critique/review artifact or critique reference. I therefore reviewed the complete admitted proposal and catalogs and checked the relevant cited public source bodies directly; this proposal does not attribute corrections to an unattached critique. The reviewed source responses had the same body SHA-256 values as the frozen catalog entries. The source and lead catalogs are explicitly adopted from those compatible same-arm references; the witness catalog below preserves the predecessor's arithmetic witness and adds one newly executed candidate component check.

The main revisions are: (1) the current Zarr latest-release endpoint still identifies v3.4.0, published before PR #4356 merged, so no released package in the checked evidence is established to contain the fix; (2) the merged implementation avoids full-grid allocations in normal sparse-indexer operation but legacy dense compatibility attributes still allocate per-chunk arrays when accessed; and (3) napari's graphical layer transform UI includes rotation in 2D, so it must be view state unless the application creates and records a derived asset operation. The proposed viewer, file support and resource targets remain unverified as an integrated application.

## What public sources establish

**Format and coordinates — external facts.** OME-NGFF v0.5 names axes and units, requires axis order to match array dimensions, and defines multiscale transforms as an ordered sequence of identity, scale and translation transforms. Its image subset covers two to five dimensions with spatial axes and optional time/channel/custom axes. Its label convention describes integer label arrays nested with a related image and imposes axes and relationship requirements [S1, §§2.1, 2.3–2.6]. This is a useful interchange subset, not a general rotated/sheared affine or a definition of this application's pixel-center convention. Store the application's complete transform and convention separately; emit NGFF transform metadata only when it faithfully represents them.

**TIFF access — external facts.** Tifffile 2026.9.20 documents selected-page/tile access and `return_as="zarr"` views, including a slice of a tiled pyramid and a Dask array view. Its release/module documentation describes a large but incomplete TIFF-family subset and codecs that require imagecodecs [S2, release body; S3, module documentation/examples]. These are mechanisms for bounded reads on appropriate tiled/segmented layouts. A lazy wrapper does not bound an arbitrary compressed strip or tile: the reader must decode the requested segment. The pinned module also documents a Zarr 3.4.0 dependency for Zarr-backed access. No cited source establishes an RSS ceiling, cancellation latency, or support for every TIFF-like instrument format.

**Zarr issue → fix → regression coverage — external facts and limits.** Issue #4174 reports that three points written by `set_coordinate_selection` to an array of shape `(10,000,000, 13,854)` with chunks `(1,1)` cause an `np.bincount` allocation reported as 1.01 TiB; the same report says basic and orthogonal examples work [S4, issue body]. PR #4356 links the issue and merged on 2026-09-28 at `cd1e5b3aaabf969d5edef18b8d62bf98e3439beb` [S5, merge metadata and body]. At that exact commit, `sorted_run_ends` groups sorted selected chunk IDs with memory proportional to the selected values, and the integer-array indexer uses occupied chunk IDs and run ends instead of a count array for every chunk [S6, lines 746–760, 812–838]. Its regression tests exercise an unsorted sparse write/read with duplicates on a 2^44-chunk array and indexer projections on a 2^62-chunk grid, including several orderings [S7, lines 1406–1487]. The PR reports 1,195 passing tests in its environment; this stage did not run that suite. Accessing compatibility attributes such as `chunk_nitems` or `chunk_nitems_cumsum` still constructs a dense `nchunks` array at the merge pin [S6, lines 851–859]; callers must avoid those attributes on huge grids.

The checked GitHub latest-release endpoint still identifies Zarr-Python v3.4.0, published 2026-09-15; the fix merged later [S8, release metadata; S5]. The fix is therefore **not established in a released version** by these sources. This does not say that all Zarr reads fail. It does show that chunking alone does not guarantee bounded memory and that the exact selection API matters. For the first version, avoid Zarr coordinate/index writes for masks at v3.4.0. If an implementation requires this route, either gate it on a later maintained release that passes the regression cases, or evaluate the exact merge commit in a separately locked prototype; do not ship a floating development branch.

**Viewer and lazy scheduling — external facts.** Napari 0.5.6 documents Image, Labels, Points and Shapes layers, n-dimensional navigation, layer visibility/blending, keyboard interaction modes and scale/translation. Its layer transform tool also supports interactive rotation in a 2D display; this transforms the displayed layer and must not silently redefine saved data coordinates [S9, “Layers at a glance,” “Layer mode,” “Scaling layers,” “Translating layers”]. Napari documents possible memory errors for very large 3D rendering [S9, “3D rendering of images”]. Its Dask tutorial shows lazy per-file reads and on-demand processing for collections, and gives a reshape example in which a slice request can trigger 600 reads instead of one [S10, “Side note regarding higher-dimensional datasets,” “Using Dask and napari to process & view large datasets”]. These are viewer/scheduler precedents, not a single 3 GB file benchmark, process-RSS result or guarantee of cancellation.

**Project transactions — external facts.** SQLite documents all-or-none transactions for its rollback-journal mode under assumptions about filesystem locking, flushes and file operations [S11, §§1–3, 9]. WAL requires processes using one live database to share a host; SQLite states it does not work over a network filesystem. A live database's `-wal` file is part of persistent state and must stay with the database if copied [S12, §§1, 4]. Neither journal mode makes a multi-file application bundle or shared-storage directory rename atomic.

## Independent implementation precedents

1. **Tifffile and Zarr are separate access mechanisms.** Tifffile maps TIFF pages/segments and can expose tiled data through a Zarr-backed view; Zarr supplies chunked n-dimensional indexing. A file-format adapter and an indexing engine have distinct, separately testable responsibilities [S2, S3, S6]. This supports a narrow reader interface, but the issue chain shows why mask-writing code must test the exact Zarr selection route. It does not bound one decoded TIFF segment or validate the app's cache.
2. **Napari and Dask are a viewer plus lazy scheduler.** Napari contributes inspectable image and annotation layers and multidimensional navigation; Dask contributes deferred block/file work. The tutorial demonstrates how to defer reads and warns that axis reshaping can amplify reads [S9, S10]. The pair does not provide this proposal's project durability, source identity, relocation checks, calibration override audit or export provenance. Preserve axis mapping and use a planned slice rather than reshaping dimensions blindly.
3. **OME-NGFF v0.5 is an independent interchange precedent.** It describes axes, units, multiscales and related integer label images without depending on the chosen viewer or local project database [S1]. Adopt only the tested subset and retain an application sidecar for the full transform and metadata the selected NGFF fields cannot carry.

## Architecture and support boundary

**Product choices.** The app owns project/asset identifiers, original metadata capture, parser/version, raw-to-view axis mapping, calibration overrides, source digests, derived-asset lineage, coordinate conversion, annotations, undo/redo and export manifests. Napari is a view/editor adapter, not the database or coordinate authority. Keep its layer transforms transient: channel visibility, contrast mapping, slice/time, pan/zoom and an exploratory rotation are view state. A user-requested crop, reorientation or resample is a separate operation, creates a new derived asset, and records its output-to-parent map. If napari cannot preserve the application coordinate mapping through draw/save/reopen/export, use a small Qt/VisPy 2D slice canvas instead; that alternative gives direct control but makes the team responsible for layer blending, point/shape interaction, keyboard navigation, rendering and accessibility. Prototype one path only.

Implement a `SourceReader` interface returning the original metadata bytes or lossless capture, parsed metadata and parser version, raw axes/order/sizes/dtype, calibration candidates, warnings/support status, and cancellable region reads. First adapters: tifffile 2026.9.20 for a fixture-defined local OME-TIFF/plain-TIFF subset; Zarr-Python 3.4.0 for a local OME-Zarr v0.5 subset; optionally Dask as a scheduler without changing the recorded dimensions. Avoid eager whole-image conversion for display. Record every reshape, rechunk, pyramid selection or proxy generation as explicit mapping/operation; do not infer axis meaning from dimension length.

**Small-team support boundary — product choice.** Support only local readable filesystems and tested fixtures: (a) OME-TIFF with one explicitly selected logical series and a whitelisted codec/layout; (b) local OME-Zarr v0.5 stores with 2–5 declared dimensions, interpretable axes and a selected multiscale level; (c) plain TIFF only where dimensions/calibration are unambiguous or the user explicitly maps them. Begin with `uint8`, `uint16`, `int16`, `uint32`, `int32`, `float32` and `float64`; keep singleton axes. Test the exact codecs, metadata variants and shapes; do not imply universal tifffile, OME-TIFF or OME-Zarr compatibility. Initially exclude remote stores, proprietary readers, multi-file stitching, unknown/complex/packed dtypes, ambiguous series, arbitrary unsupported axis layouts and guaranteed whole-volume rendering. The format name alone is not an acceptance test.

If parsing or decoding fails, keep a project asset record in `UNSUPPORTED` or metadata-only state with original metadata, selected path, parser version and readable reason. Do not create spatial annotations until axes and coordinate interpretation are explicit. Offer the user a series choice, explicit axis/calibration mapping, metadata-only import, or cancel/remove the failed record. Never rewrite the source. A proxy is a user-requested derived asset with space estimate, progress, cancel and lineage; it does not replace the original.

## Minimum workflow

1. **Create and import.** Create a local project and add multiple source assets read-only. Record an `IMPORTING` row before long work. Parse/store metadata and allow inspection while a cancellable streaming SHA-256 is `PENDING`; do not bind export/relink to an unverified digest. Detect a source mutation during hashing and retry or report it. For a directory Zarr store, hash a sorted manifest of relative key, length and per-object digest, then hash that manifest. Preserve byte-exact original metadata (or a declared lossless capture), parser/version and every user override separately.
2. **Inspect.** Show file/container, series/level, original axes and order, shape, dtype, candidate pixel/time units, origin/orientation, parser and digest state, provenance and warnings. Unknown axes/calibration are visible. Ask for an explicit axis-role, unit, origin, spacing or orientation correction and record value, author, time and reason without changing source metadata. Missing calibration remains unknown; show `px` where appropriate rather than inventing micrometres.
3. **Navigate/display.** Browse selected channels, z plane and time; display the sample dtype and physical spacing; identify original and derived layers with text labels. Save per-channel visibility, window/level, LUT/gamma, blending, current slice/time and viewport as view state. These alter display buffers only, never source samples. A displayed projection/composite is also a view, not a derived sample asset.
4. **Annotate.** Support points, polygons and integer masks. Provide keyboard navigation for channels, axes, slices and time; reusable named/versioned annotation styles; and undo/redo for annotation edits. Show scope explicitly: selected `(t,z)` plane, volume at one time, or whole acquisition. Record channel applicability as all or explicit channel IDs. Each edit is transactional; masks update only affected immutable chunks.
5. **Save/close.** Commit metadata/geometry/history in a local project transaction. At close, finish or cancel outstanding jobs and validate project references. Do not allow concurrent writers to one shared project database in v1.
6. **Exchange/reopen.** Export a closed, verified bundle to ordinary shared storage and reopen on a second workstation. Portable mode copies source bytes unchanged; reference mode stores relative/external locators and digests. If a path is missing, offer relocation and reconnect only after digest match. A same-path mismatch stays a different asset; preserve the prior annotations and ask for an explicit, recorded replacement mapping.
7. **Export/verify.** Export an annotation table, a transparent overlay for a chosen plane/time and a small crop image. Show source asset/digest, coordinate frame, units, scope, crop bounds, output dtype/format, transforms, render settings and fields/metadata omitted. Verify exported samples/coordinates and confirm source bytes/digest remain unchanged.

## Coordinate and annotation contract

**Product choice — stable coordinate frame.** Preserve every raw array axis, native name/order, size and unit. Store a reviewed mapping for each raw axis to `T`, `C`, `Z`, `Y`, `X` or named unknown; do not infer channel/time from length. Application spatial coordinates are continuous zero-based voxel-center coordinates `(x,y,z)`: integer indices identify source sample centers. Time and channel remain explicit selectors/scopes, not spatial axes. Record the convention and row/column vector convention in the project and export.

Store a double-precision homogeneous `H_source_xyz_to_physical` mapping column vector `[x,y,z,1]` to `[physical_x,physical_y,physical_z,1]`, with declared physical frame/basis, units, origin and source-to-application axis permutation/directions. Keep the complete raw source transform metadata unmodified beside it. If calibration is absent, record physical calibration as unknown and use pixel units for displayed indices. Never substitute the viewport transform for this mapping. OME-NGFF v0.5 scale/translation metadata can express straight-axis calibration; if rotation/reflection/shear cannot be represented faithfully, keep the exact app matrix authoritative in the project/sidecar and label any NGFF view as incomplete [S1, §2.3]. Reject singular or contradictory transforms until the user supplies an explicit correction.

An annotation stores immutable annotation ID; target asset ID and verified source digest; geometry type and values in the declared asset voxel frame; fixed and spanning axes (including `t` and `z` scope); channel scope; category; style ID/version; author/time; and edit-history reference. Never store screen coordinates. A whole-acquisition note still names its asset and explicit scope. A mask uses integer labels and records chunk shape/order and coordinate frame.

For a crop starting at child-to-parent source offset `c`, `H_child = H_parent × T(c)`, where `T(c)` maps child voxel indices to parent indices. For resampling, store the complete output-to-parent mapping, output shape/dtype, interpolation, fill value and software/version. Create a distinct derived asset and lineage record. Map new annotations back only when the map is valid and invertible; otherwise attach them to the derived asset. Never move an annotation silently after a transformation.

**Executed coordinate witness.** The predecessor witness W1 checks 2D scale-plus-translation crop rebasing only. This revision also ran W2, a small candidate-authored 3D affine/axis-order/crop arithmetic check. It maps raw `T,C,Z,Y,X` indices to application `x,y,z` and confirms that a rotated, anisotropic affine gives the same physical point before and after crop. Both are isolated arithmetic checks, not tests of OME parsing, reader axis order, napari drawing, persistence, resampling or export. Code, input, expected result and receipts are in `witnesses.json`.

## Project storage, identity and recovery

**Product choice.** Keep a local project directory with versioned SQLite database, immutable content-addressed mask/derived objects and a manifest. Use one local writer and SQLite rollback-journal mode initially; do not edit a live project on shared storage. The six researchers exchange closed project bundles. A path is a locator, not identity: use an asset UUID plus source SHA-256; for directory stores use a deterministic key/byte manifest digest. Hashing multi-gigabyte inputs takes time, so expose progress/cancel and keep identity `PENDING` until complete. On relocation, verify content, not just path or filename.

Write new mask chunks to unique temporary names; verify and flush the chunk before transactionally changing the SQLite pointer and appending undo history. A crash before pointer commit leaves an orphan safe to collect later; a committed pointer must refer to immutable verified bytes. Stage imports with `IMPORTING`/`FAILED`/`READY`, and never present a partial import as complete. On reopen, validate the database and reachable blobs, retain valid annotation records if another object is missing, and report what could not be recovered. Garbage-collect orphans only after consistency checks.

For exchange, build a unique staging sibling, write manifest and hashes, copy/verify all members, then publish as a new versioned bundle. Keep the previous ready bundle. A ready marker written last is a fallback where directory replacement semantics are uncertain; test rename, flush and locking on each actual shared-storage type before depending on them. Do not promise atomicity across SQLite plus masks plus copied image files. SQLite transactions protect only their database boundary and rely on filesystem/OS behavior [S11]. Avoid WAL for shared-storage live use [S12]. If WAL is ever enabled locally, close/checkpoint cleanly and package every persistent WAL member together.

Offer two explicit modes. A **reference bundle** is small and quick and stores digests plus relative/external paths; the image may be offline and require verified relocation. A **portable bundle** copies original bytes unchanged with project state and is self-contained, at the cost of extra storage and copy time (a project may add several gigabytes). Show mode, storage estimate and copy progress. Derived proxies/crops are always separate assets marked derived. Build any exported bundle without overwriting the prior known-good version; after interruption retain a discoverable incomplete staging record and a recoverable last committed project.

## Export fidelity and derived operations

- **Display-only changes:** window/level, LUT/gamma, channel selection/blend, slice/time, pan/zoom and an exploratory napari layer transform are view state. They never rewrite the original sample array or redefine the annotation frame.
- **Data operations:** crop, orientation change, resample, sample conversion, proxy generation and rechunking create a new asset with parent ID/digest, operation ID/type, input/output axes/shape/dtype, exact coordinate map, interpolation/fill where applicable, software/version, parameters, actor and time. Preserve original source bytes read-only.
- **Annotation table:** canonical versioned JSONL holds complete geometry/scope/style/provenance; a CSV convenience export has stable columns and JSON geometry/scope fields. A CSV alone cannot carry editable mask pixels. Export masks as separate integer arrays with an explicit coordinate/shape reference; use an OME-NGFF v0.5 label group only when its axes and image relationship conform, otherwise export a described companion mask plus authoritative sidecar [S1, §2.6].
- **Overlay:** transparent RGBA PNG for a chosen channel/slice/time, with source identity, plane, palette, rasterization and affine in a manifest. It is a rendered picture, not editable geometry and not a lossless annotation format.
- **Small derived image:** stream a selected crop through bounded reads. Default to source sample values and dtype with no display LUT/window operation and no resampling; record source voxel bounds and child affine. Write calibrated OME-TIFF only when metadata can be expressed without loss, and always include app sidecar. State which metadata consumers may ignore. Verify output coordinates/dtype/samples and source digest before/after; if exact preservation or verification fails, report it instead of claiming it.

## Responsiveness and accessibility targets

**Engineering inference.** A 3 GB source might be browsable within 16 GB if reads, decoded blocks, in-flight work, display buffers and caches are all bounded. Tifffile/Zarr/Dask/napari document useful mechanisms, not this application's memory or latency. A giant TIFF segment or Zarr chunk can break the bound even with lazy indexing. Napari's large-volume 3D warning argues for 2D slices as the reliable first view [S9].

**Proposed validation targets (UNEXECUTED).** Prototype a 512 MiB decoded-block LRU, reject or explicitly proxy any source segment/chunk above 64 MiB, cap decode concurrency at two (visible read plus at most one prefetch), and bound display buffers. The nominal cache/in-flight/buffer budget is about 768 MiB before library and UI overhead; test actual process RSS rather than calling that a guarantee. Proposed first gate: no more than 1 GiB RSS growth above idle while navigating a supported 3 GB fixture. Record cold/warm slice p50/p95, maximum decoded segment, bytes read, cache behavior and annotation update responsiveness on the target 8-core/16 GB class machine. Cancellation checks between chunks/segments; if a codec cannot stop mid-segment, show progress and tell the user cancellation takes effect after that decode. Expose progress and cancel for hashing, import/proxy, save and export. An oversized unit should warn before decode and offer explicit derived proxy with disk estimate or metadata-only unsupported state.

Keyboard operation must cover project open/save, channel/slice/time navigation, point/polygon/mask tools, style selection, undo/redo, dialogs, export and recovery. Show shortcuts and allow remapping. Mark original/derived with text and icons, not color alone. Use legible metadata, keyboard focus and screen-reader announcements for progress/errors; name the failing file/axis and provide recovery choices. A traceback alone is not useful feedback.

## Validation before a useful release (all proposed/UNEXECUTED)

1. **Format fixtures:** OME-TIFF, plain TIFF and OME-Zarr fixtures across supported axis permutations, singleton axes, integer/float samples, known/missing/contradictory calibration, multiple series, unsupported codec and oversized segment/chunk. Compare parser output to hand-authored fixture expectations; unknown axes stay visible.
2. **3 GB bounded access:** on target workstations test one tiled/segmented 3 GB TIFF and one chunked image plus an oversized-segment control. Measure RSS, actual bytes read, segment/chunk maxima, cold/warm slice p50/p95 and time-to-cancel. Confirm there is no source-sized second allocation. This is the brief's performance gate; the cited tutorials are not substitutes.
3. **Coordinates:** round-trip known voxel centers across raw axis orders, units, anisotropic scale, origin, direction/reflection/rotation and crop. Compare physical coordinates before/after; reject singular/contradictory metadata; compare app sidecar to any emitted NGFF/TIFF metadata. W1 and W2 check arithmetic only.
4. **Display/operation separation:** alter contrast/LUT/channel settings for integer and float fixtures and prove source bytes/samples do not change. Compare crop output dtype and every selected sample; verify a resample has new identity, mapping, interpolation and derived badge.
5. **Identity/relocation:** close, move bundle and image, relink byte-identical content, reject same-path changed bytes, mutate/delete one Zarr key and validate directory-manifest behavior. Measure hash duration and test concurrent source mutation detection.
6. **Crash recovery:** inject interruption during metadata import, mask object write, SQLite pointer transaction, bundle copy, verification and publish. Reopen to check committed rows, reachable object hashes, incomplete operation state and previous ready bundle. Test actual local and shared filesystems for lock/flush/rename/marker behavior.
7. **Export round trip:** verify JSONL/CSV scope/coordinates, mask labels, overlay plane/palette, crop bounds/samples/dtype and calibration sidecar. Reopen exported files with a second supported reader and explicitly report metadata it ignores. Rehash original data.
8. **Zarr path:** if any app annotation uses coordinate-selection/indexer APIs, run S7 regression cases against the actual locked release and add application memory tests; also ensure no path touches dense compatibility attributes at scale. Until a released pin is demonstrated fixed, use chunk/block writes avoiding the coordinate path or defer that feature.
9. **Usability/accessibility:** keyboard-only create/import/inspect/navigate/annotate/undo/save/close/reopen/export; dialog focus, screen-reader labels, legible warnings, and original/derived distinction without color. Observe all six researchers' first-use workflow before widening support.

## Opportunities, alternatives and unresolved dependencies

**Useful opportunity.** OME-NGFF v0.5 image/label interchange can reduce lock-in for compatible axes and integer masks. Preserve the complete app affine, provenance and unsupported scope in the app's versioned sidecar. Reusable instrument import profiles are another follow-up, but only apply to matching signatures and must preview raw metadata and exact overrides. Explicit rechunked/pyramidal proxies may rescue oversized or slow local assets while leaving original bytes untouched.

**Plausible alternative.** A custom Qt/VisPy 2D slice canvas gives the group direct control over coordinate transforms, memory and draw tools if napari's transform conventions or memory gates fail. It costs more interaction/rendering/accessibility engineering; decide after a minimal napari draw-save-reopen-export spike rather than implementing both.

**Portability alternative.** Reference bundles save shared-storage space but depend on locator/relink and digest checks. Portable copies work offline and archive more simply but duplicate multi-gigabyte sources. Keep both explicit and publish only closed immutable versions.

**Deferred leads, not minimum obligations.** Concurrent editing/cloud sync would need conflict and provenance rules. Automatic segmentation and registration need reviewable, distinguishable derived results and validated transforms. GPU rendering is optional and cannot be the baseline. Bio-Formats or other plugins may expand the curated format matrix but require the same metadata, coordinate, bounded-read and failure guarantees; none makes compatibility universal. Retain these ideas for later evidence, not as v1 promises.

Critical open gates are napari/Qt/Dask/tifffile/Zarr version compatibility; preserving source-axis mapping through viewer draw/save/export; per-codec TIFF segment size and interruption behavior; calibration/orientation semantics for real instruments; supportable OME-Zarr v0.5 subset; release availability of the Zarr sparse-index fix; and the group's actual filesystem behavior for lock/flush/rename. Until a spike and fixtures settle those dependencies, keep the support matrix and 3D view narrow. No complete application or integrated performance/recovery/export behavior has been tested here.